use @piton/belay

from ../../lib/PitonSkillBase import PitonSkillBase

// Shared base for the crux UI skills. It is abstract, so it never
// compiles or is emitted on its own. It holds the rules every UI skill
// follows for the HTML pages it saves, the gallery's large view, how
// a saved page is shown to the user, and how the specification points
// at saved pages. Rules that belong to one skill, such as numbered
// mockup rounds or the style library, stay in that skill. Shared
// guidance lives in named properties, which inherit cleanly and are
// serialized as sections after each concrete skill's own prompt.
export abstract anchor CruxUiBase extends PitonSkillBase:
    savedPages:
        Every page a UI skill saves, whether a mockup, a style sample
        or a gallery, is a single self-contained HTML file with inline
        CSS. Use JavaScript only for a gallery's controls or to show an
        interaction that cannot be seen without it. Load nothing from
        the network, so the files open offline and look the same years
        later.

        Piton reads only .pi files, so saved pages never affect piton
        check or piton build. If the project tethers its source root as
        a package, the pages travel with it, which is intended.

        A saved page is a picture of the UI, not the implementation, so
        never write it into the application code and never copy it
        there. Build it from the project, not from generic
        placeholders. Use realistic content shaped like the project's
        real data, never lorem ipsum.

    styleCatalogue:
        The crux package ships a catalogue of over four hundred worked
        visual styles, light and dark, as Piton source with one anchor
        file per style, named after the style. Each file, such as
        EditorialGuideStyle.pi, describes one style in exact values,
        with the same properties a style library has, such as number,
        name, idea, base, screen, palette, type, layout, spacing,
        density, corners, borders, shadows, icons, focus and tone. The
        files are in the catalogue/styles folder of the
        installed crux package, usually under the project's tethers
        folder. Find them by file name, since the package folder's name
        can differ between projects. If they are not there, work without
        them and do not mention it.

        The catalogue is never imported into the project, so it is not
        in the project's compiled reference tree. Search its files as
        text. Search the name and idea lines first for words that match
        what you need, such as dark, serif, dense, terminal or the kind
        of screen, then open only the few style files that fit. Search
        the other properties for an exact treatment, such as a
        monospace type stack or square corners. Every catalogue style
        was drawn for a sample work suite, so its content words belong
        to that sample.

        Use the catalogue as reference and inspiration, never as a
        source to copy. Adapt what you take to the project's own
        screens, data, design rules and language. Never import a
        catalogue anchor into the project, and never cite a catalogue
        style as a style in the project's specification. The project's
        own specification and style library always win over the
        catalogue.

    largeView:
        A gallery lets the user dive into any page it shows. Clicking a
        card opens a large view of that page inside the gallery. The
        large view has a width switch for wide, tablet and 375px phone
        sizes, previous and next controls, a link that opens the page
        file on its own, and Escape to return to the grid.

    showingPages:
        After writing a gallery, show it. Give the user its path
        relative to the working directory, and open it with whatever
        preview the host actually provides, such as a browser tool or
        the system's file opener. Never assume a particular tool
        exists. If iframes do not load from the file system in the
        user's browser, suggest serving the folder with a simple local
        static server and give the exact command.

    referencingPages:
        The specification points at saved pages with paths relative to
        the project root, where piton.config.pi lives, such as
        spec/surface/Card.mockups/round-2/3.html. Use project-root
        paths because a Markdown link written in prose is plain text
        that Piton never rewrites, so a relative link would break in
        the compiled reference tree. Run piton format, piton check and
        piton build after editing the specification.

        A saved page illustrates the specification. It never replaces
        it. Everything a page settles is also written as plain
        requirements in the anchor. Someone who never opens the page
        must still be able to build the UI from the specification.
